# Copy to secrets.env (gitignored) and fill in. Every token, key and account ID
# the scripts use, and nothing else — targets are in targets.env.
#
# Read one key at a time with `secret NAME` (scripts/lib/common.sh): from the
# environment when set (CI repo secrets use these same names), else from this
# file. The file is sourced by bash in a subshell, so a value may be a lookup
# rather than sit here in the clear:
#   CLOUDFLARE_PROD_API_TOKEN=$(security find-generic-password -s wisdom-cf-prod -w)
#
# Every name carries a prefix. Loading this file must never arm a variable a
# tool reads on its own: `use_cloudflare dev|prod` (lib/common.sh) maps one
# target's pair to wrangler's CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN/CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID only
# inside a deploy, and the deploy runs its tests without them.


# --- Cloudflare: one token + account ID per account --------------------------
# dev = the wisdomproject.dev account, prod = the ops account. Both work the
# same way; nothing uses `wrangler login`. A deploy checks with `wrangler
# whoami` that the token reaches the account the ID names, and stops if not.
#
# TOKEN — create an ACCOUNT-OWNED token, not a user one, in that account:
#   dashboard -> Manage Account -> Account API Tokens -> Create Token
#             -> Create Custom Token
#   Permissions (search the picker for each):
#     Cloudflare Pages   Edit    <- both accounts
#     Account Settings   Read    <- both; REQUIRED, not optional: without it
#                                   `wrangler whoami` cannot list accounts and
#                                   every deploy refuses to run.
#     Workers            Editor  <- prod only: the research Worker (the role
#                                   system of 2026-09; "Workers Scripts" rows
#                                   are legacy). No zone permission: its custom
#                                   domain is attached in the dashboard.
#   One prod token for both Pages and Workers, on purpose — not split per
#   product (decided 2026-09-27).
#   Scope: Entire Account.  Client IP filtering: leave empty (a home IP rotates
#   and the resulting 403 is very hard to read).  TTL: an expiry is cheap — the
#   failure is a clear auth error, not silent breakage.
#
#   Account-owned (`cfat_`) rather than user-owned (`cfut_`) because the token
#   must outlive whichever person configured it. Do NOT use the "Edit Cloudflare
#   Workers" template: it does not reliably carry the Pages permission.
#
# ACCOUNT ID — 32 hex characters; the easiest source is the dashboard URL:
#   https://dash.cloudflare.com/<ACCOUNT_ID>/workers-and-pages
CLOUDFLARE_DEV_API_TOKEN=
CLOUDFLARE_DEV_ACCOUNT_ID=
CLOUDFLARE_PROD_API_TOKEN=
CLOUDFLARE_PROD_ACCOUNT_ID=


# --- Research server ----------------------------------------------------------
# Handed to wrangler under the Worker's own names: to `wrangler dev` by
# scripts/research_server/run.sh, and uploaded with every
# scripts/research_server/deploy.sh. An EMPTY key is left out, so the Worker
# keeps the value it already has — emptying a line here never clears it there.
#
# The Worker's GEMINI_API_KEY, from AI Studio -> API keys in the Google project
# that made RESEARCH_STORE (research_server/wrangler.jsonc) — a store belongs to
# one project, so the two change together. Today that is the personal project;
# it moves to the ops `wisdom-research` project with the next store
# (docs/todo/research/ingestion-node-rewrite-and-chunking-plan.md). deploy.sh
# and run.sh check the pair.
RESEARCH_GEMINI_API_KEY=
